  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Breezy with moderating temperatures today.\n\nLow pressure will meander over the Gulf of Maine and Canadian\nMaritimes today before lifting to the north and departing\ntonight. Meanwhile, high pressure centered over the Midwest will\nslowly build east.\n\nSome weak shortwave energy passing through the region early this\nmorning will result in some mid-level clouds which will\ndissipate after sunrise. With sunny skies and the pressure \ngradient tightening once again over the area, west winds will\nincrease to 15 to 20 mph with gusts up to 25 mph. Temperatures\nwill moderate somewhat compared to Saturday, with highs mostly\nin the low 60s. Winds diminish tonight.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A potentially strong coastal storm/nor'easter \nlate this week.\n\nA significant upper-level trough is forecast to evolve across \nthe eastern U.S. later this week. This should support a surface \nlow that initially tracks near the Ohio Valley, however a \nsecondary low should develop in the vicinity of the northern \nMid-Atlantic. The model guidance overall is in decent agreement \nwith the development of the strong upper-level trough, however \nthere are some differences on the handling of the embedded \nshortwaves and thus the surface low. The timing and \namplification of the trough will also have an impact on the \nsecondary surface low development and track. This will be \nimportant as it will determine where the main forcing for ascent\nbecomes established along with colder air on the west/northwest\nside of the developing coastal storm/nor'easter.\n\nThe strength of the parent upper-level trough would result in a\ncolder air mass moving in with time. This could be sufficiently\ncold enough for a rain/snow mix or even some snow across our \nfar western to northwestern zones. This would have a greater \nchance of occurrence if the secondary low develops and takes \nover instead of the initial low remaining primary and tracking \nover our area or to our west. Elsewhere, a decent amount of rain\nis possible. The amount of precipitation (rain and/or snow), \nand precipitation types for at least portions of our area, will \ndepend on the ultimate development and track of the secondary \nsurface low and the associated thermal profiles that evolve \nacross our region. The coastal impacts, including the potential \nfor stronger winds and also tidal flooding, are less certain \nbecause if the surface low tracks over our area that would \nlessen the residence time of the (stronger) onshore flow. Lots \nof uncertainty with the details this far out in time (Thursday \nthrough Friday night), therefore this time frame will continue \nto be monitored.",
